sections.body:
Take the comment-count example. There are three reasonable ways to serve it, each with a different trade-off:

```sql
-- Option 1: compute it live, every time -- always correct, costs a join+count per request
SELECT p.id, p.title, COUNT(c.id) AS comment_count
FROM posts p LEFT JOIN comments c ON c.post_id = p.id
GROUP BY p.id, p.title;

-- Option 2: a denormalized counter column, kept in sync by a trigger
ALTER TABLE posts ADD COLUMN comment_count INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;

CREATE FUNCTION bump_comment_count() RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
BEGIN
    IF TG_OP = 'INSERT' THEN
        UPDATE posts SET comment_count = comment_count + 1 WHERE id = NEW.post_id;
    ELSIF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN
        UPDATE posts SET comment_count = comment_count - 1 WHERE id = OLD.post_id;
    END IF;
    RETURN NULL;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER comments_count_trigger
AFTER INSERT OR DELETE ON comments
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION bump_comment_count();
```

Option 1 is the normalized default: correct by construction, no extra column to keep in sync, but potentially expensive if run often against a large `comments` table. Option 2 makes reads on `posts` cheap, since `comment_count` is just a column with no join. The price is the redundancy normalization warns about: `comment_count` now duplicates a fact that `comments` already implies, and only the trigger keeps it right. A bug in the trigger, or a bulk `DELETE` run while the trigger is disabled for a migration, can desynchronize it without any error. The trigger also makes every comment insert update the parent post's row, so a post receiving many simultaneous comments serializes on that row lock.

A third option, a **materialized view**, sits in between: it caches an expensive aggregate's result physically and is refreshed on a schedule (`REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ...`) instead of being kept live by a trigger on every write. It fits a query that is expensive but doesn't need the latest write, such as a daily analytics dashboard. The right choice depends on how stale the count may be and on how often comments are written compared with how often posts are read. No answer is correct independent of those numbers.

PostgreSQL's `JSONB` type stores semi-structured data efficiently and lets you index into it and query it, which makes it a good fit for attributes that are sparse or variable across rows: product attributes that differ by category (a shirt has `size` and `color`; a laptop has `ram_gb` and `screen_inches`), where giving every possible attribute its own nullable column would leave most columns NULL for most rows and need a migration each time a new category adds an attribute.

```sql
CREATE TABLE products (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    category TEXT NOT NULL,
    attributes JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'
);

CREATE INDEX idx_products_attributes ON products USING GIN (attributes);

INSERT INTO products (name, category, attributes) VALUES
    ('Blue T-Shirt', 'apparel', '{"size": "M", "color": "blue"}'),
    ('ThinkBook 14', 'laptop', '{"ram_gb": 16, "screen_inches": 14}');

-- Query using the containment operator, backed by the GIN index
SELECT name FROM products WHERE attributes @> '{"color": "blue"}';

-- Extract a specific field with ->>
SELECT name, attributes->>'ram_gb' AS ram FROM products WHERE category = 'laptop';
```

The `GIN` index makes containment (`@>`) and key-existence (`?`) queries on the JSONB column efficient, instead of a full scan that parses every row's JSON. But JSONB has costs that make it a poor default for data that isn't sparse or variable. You can't declare a `NOT NULL` or `CHECK` constraint on one key inside a JSONB blob the way you can on a column, no foreign key can point at a value nested inside one, and every query against a JSONB field is a little more verbose and less self-documenting than a plainly named, plainly typed column. The test to apply before reaching for JSONB: is this attribute set open-ended and varying per row (a good fit), or is it a fixed, known, structured relationship that JSONB is being used to avoid modeling (a misuse, covered next)?

