-- Step 1 (migration + deploy): add the new column, old column untouched
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email_address TEXT;

-- Step 2 (app deploy only): write to both `email` and `email_address`
-- on every insert/update; still read from `email`.

-- Step 3 (backfill): copy existing values into the new column
UPDATE users SET email_address = email WHERE email_address IS NULL;

-- Step 4 (app deploy only): read from `email_address` instead of `email`;
-- keep writing both for safety during this transition.

-- Step 5 (migration + deploy, once nothing reads/writes `email` anymore):
ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN email;

sections.body:
A foreign key's `ON DELETE` clause says what Postgres does to a child row when the parent row it references is deleted. There are four standard behaviors.

```sql
CREATE TABLE posts (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    author_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES authors(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,
    title TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE post_tags (
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES tags(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    PRIMARY KEY (post_id, tag_id)
);

CREATE TABLE comments (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    user_id BIGINT REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
    body TEXT NOT NULL
);
```

**RESTRICT** refuses the DELETE on the parent with an error if any child row still references it, so you must delete or reassign the children first. When no `ON DELETE` clause is given, Postgres's default is **NO ACTION**, which differs from RESTRICT in one respect: RESTRICT's check can never be deferred to the end of a transaction, while NO ACTION's can be if the constraint is declared `DEFERRABLE`. For the great majority of schemas, which don't use deferrable constraints, the two behave the same and block the delete with a foreign key violation. **CASCADE** deletes every child row referencing the deleted parent, propagating the delete downward. **SET NULL** keeps the child row but sets its foreign key column to NULL, severing the link while keeping the row; it only works if the column is nullable. A fourth, less common option, **SET DEFAULT**, sets the foreign key column to its declared default value (instead of NULL) when the parent is deleted, which needs a default that points at a row that exists.

**Soft deletion** sidesteps the CASCADE/RESTRICT/SET NULL decision for a table by never issuing a hard `DELETE` against it. Instead a `deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ` column (NULL for active rows, the deletion time for 'deleted' ones) marks a row as logically gone while it stays in the table.

```sql
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ;

-- "Deleting" a user:
UPDATE users SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = 42;

-- Every query that should only see active users needs this filter:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

-- A unique constraint needs to apply only to non-deleted rows,
-- otherwise a re-registration with the same email is blocked forever:
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email_active ON users (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;
```

Because the `users` row never disappears, every foreign key referencing it stays valid with no CASCADE/RESTRICT/SET NULL decision at the database level: a comment's `user_id` keeps pointing at a row that still exists, now flagged as deleted. That solves the orphaning problem but brings other costs. Every query against `users` (and often everything joined to it) must remember to filter out soft-deleted rows, which is easy to forget in a new query and becomes a correctness bug when it is. A plain `UNIQUE` constraint on `email` would block a legitimate new signup reusing a deleted account's email, which is why the partial unique index above is needed. And 'deleting' no longer frees storage or removes the data, a problem when legal or privacy rules require data to be erased on request. Soft deletion is a common, legitimate pattern and suits user-facing 'undo my delete' features, but it is a trade-off, not a strictly better alternative to choosing an ON DELETE strategy. Many systems use both, hard-deleting some tables with carefully chosen FK strategies and soft-deleting others where recoverability matters more.

examples.code:
CREATE TABLE authors (id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE users   (id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);

CREATE TABLE posts (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    author_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES authors(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,
    title TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE comments (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    user_id BIGINT REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
    body TEXT NOT NULL
);

-- Author still has a post -- RESTRICT blocks this delete
DELETE FROM authors WHERE id = 1;
-- ERROR:  update or delete on table "authors" violates foreign key constraint

-- Deleting a post CASCADEs to its comments -- they're gone too
DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = 5;
-- comments where post_id = 5 are deleted automatically

-- Deleting a user SET NULLs their comments' user_id -- the comments remain
DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 7;
-- comments where user_id = 7 now have user_id = NULL, body unchanged

-- If ALL of these used ON DELETE CASCADE (not the mixed strategy above):
CREATE TABLE posts (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    author_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES authors(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,  -- risky
    title TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE comments (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    body TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE post_tags (
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES tags(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    PRIMARY KEY (post_id, tag_id)
);

-- One statement, unbounded downstream damage:
DELETE FROM authors WHERE id = 1;
-- silently also deletes: every post by author 1, every comment on
-- every one of those posts (regardless of WHO wrote the comment),
-- and every post_tags row for every one of those posts

CREATE TABLE users (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    email TEXT NOT NULL,
    deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ
);

-- Unique only among ACTIVE (non-deleted) users
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email_active ON users (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

INSERT INTO users (email) VALUES ('amy@example.com');

UPDATE users SET deleted_at = now() WHERE email = 'amy@example.com';

-- Succeeds: the old row is soft-deleted, so this doesn't collide with it
INSERT INTO users (email) VALUES ('amy@example.com');

-- Every ordinary query must remember to exclude soft-deleted rows:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'amy@example.com' AND deleted_at IS NULL;

